WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.430
What is your paper about?

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:12.450
To find an answer to this question, you first
have to consider the following question as

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.110
well.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:17.740
First and foremost, is what you want to write
about, worth it?

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.770
In other words, you have to ask yourself the
“so what” test.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:26.090
What will be the contribution of the paper
to this field you're interested in?

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:31.070
If you write this paper, what will change
in the way academics see the field?

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:35.370
What will you bring to the table that hasn't
been said before in those terms?

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:39.600
To answer this question you have to build
your literature review, the core elements

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.990
in an academic paper.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:46.720
It will be tantamount to know what your contribution
may be to your field of interest.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.629
The second question is, with whom should I
discuss in order to make sure that you pass

00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:52.710
the “so what” test?

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.059
How do you ensure that you pass the “so
what” test?

00:00:56.059 --> 00:00:59.550
This part is as scientific as it is political.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.470
You have to be sure that the vocabulary that
you use in the frame of your proposition will

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:08.020
be coherent with the way the reviewer will
make sense of the issue you're raising.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.280
To ensure that, identify colleagues and member
of your network that can help you position

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.759
the paper in the terms that you have chosen.

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:19.530
Finally, the third question you have to ask
yourself is, what kind of paper do you want

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.520
to write?

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.130
Academics paper can have different goals.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.689
Whether your goal is theoretical, empirical,
or a methodological one, the design of the

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:30.869
paper will be different.

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:35.100
As the contributions and aim of these papers
are different, so will be the setting, the

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.049
methodologies, and the research question.

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:41.939
Your contribution will be impacted by this
orientation.

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:45.820
For instance, if you're looking to publish
in medical journal, for example like the New

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:51.490
England Journal of Medicine, which is oriented
toward clinician as well as researchers.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:56.429
The journal will not be looking for the same
contribution as a pure biological oriented

00:01:56.429 --> 00:01:59.249
journal, for example Nature or Cell.

00:01:59.249 --> 00:02:03.409
All these questions and elements are linked
to your research question.

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:07.439
The research question is the main question
raised in your paper, and the one you bring

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:09.450
elements of answer to in the paper.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:14.130
In short, you have to understand the field
you’re researching in.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:18.220
Know what you can contribute to it, and know
how to communicate your contribution to this

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.470
academic community.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:24.600
You have to position your paper so that the
community of researchers you are specialized

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.750
in the theme you have chosen will accept it.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.010
Many researchers struggle when it comes to
writing literature review for their research

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.090
paper.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:39.730
A literature review is a comprehensive overview
of all the knowledge available on a specific

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:41.780
topic till date.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:46.040
When you decide on a research topic, usually
the first step you take in the direction of

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:50.480
conducting research is learn more about the
previous research published on the topic,

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:56.070
and this eventually translates into literature
review when you write your research paper.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:03:00.570
Literature review is one of the pillars on
which your research idea stands since it provides

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:08.200
context, relevance, and background to the
research problem you are exploring.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.470
Literature review essentially involves two
steps:

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:16.010
Surveying and critically reading the existing
literature: this step is commonly referred

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.400
to as experimental literature review.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.730
Summarizing and actually penning down the
gist of your review in an organized manner:

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.890
this is known as theoretical review.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:31.590
Literature review could be a part of a dissertation
or research article and a stand-alone literature

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.280
review.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.140
Let us look at this in more detail.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.690
Every research report, thesis, or research
article begins with an introduction to the

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.900
topic of research.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.180
This forms the literature review for the article.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.180
The main purpose of the review is to introduce
the readers to the need for conducting the

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.990
said research.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:58.510
A literature review should begin with a thorough
literature search using the main keywords

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:03.920
in relevant online databases such as Google
Scholar, PubMed, etc.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:09.500
Once all the relevant literature has been
gathered, it should be organized as follows:

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:13.420
Background literature about the broad research
topic to introduce the readers to the field

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.160
of study.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:20.320
Recent progress on the study topic which can
be organized thematically or chronologically.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.180
Ideally, separate themes should be discussed
in a chronological manner to describe how

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:30.729
research in the field has evolved over time
and to highlight the progress in the field.

00:04:30.729 --> 00:04:35.550
The review should include a comparison and
contrast of different studies.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:39.460
Discussing the controversial aspects helps
to identify the main gaps that need to be

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.349
worked upon.

00:04:41.349 --> 00:04:44.949
This is essential for defining the problem
statement of the study and highlighting the

00:04:44.949 --> 00:04:48.330
significance of the research under question.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:52.419
Once a problem statement has been defined,
the strengths and pitfalls of other studies

00:04:52.419 --> 00:04:56.150
that have tackled the problem statement should
be discussed.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:00.419
This is important for outlining the need and
novelty of the research.

00:05:00.419 --> 00:05:05.650
A literature review should not be a mere recounting
of all the available information.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.699
It should be a critical and analytical summary
of the selected literature that guides the

00:05:09.699 --> 00:05:15.639
readers through the central theme of the research.

00:05:15.639 --> 00:05:19.599
Literature reviews can also be written as
stand-alone articles.

00:05:19.599 --> 00:05:23.770
These are not different from the literature
review sections described above; however,

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:26.770
they are not followed by experimental data.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:35.469
They basically fall into 2 broad categories:
narrative reviews and systematic reviews.

00:05:35.469 --> 00:05:39.960
Narrative reviews are theoretical discussions
of relevant information on a particular topic

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.460
and its critical analysis.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:48.270
These are mostly qualitative in nature similar
to the review sections of larger articles.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:52.310
Narrative reviews are usually organized as
follows:

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:56.030
Introduction that establishes the context
of the field of research and the topic of

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:57.529
the review.

00:05:57.529 --> 00:06:02.180
Body is normally used for describing the different
themes under the main topic by dividing them

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:04.699
into different subheadings.

00:06:04.699 --> 00:06:09.279
This section compares and contrasts published
studies and identifies gaps that have not

00:06:09.279 --> 00:06:13.120
been addressed or have been unsuccessfully
addressed.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.790
Conclusions.

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:19.410
This section differs slightly between reviews
which are part of research articles and narrative

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:20.410
reviews.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:25.409
The section describes the main conclusions
from analysis of all the current studies and

00:06:25.409 --> 00:06:28.729
puts forth further avenues for research.

00:06:28.729 --> 00:06:33.280
This section requires critical interpretation
by the author such that the review adds value

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:34.840
to existing literature.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:40.379
It should bring out ideas and hypotheses that
can explain any discrepancies and provide

00:06:40.379 --> 00:06:46.059
solutions to existing problems.

00:06:46.059 --> 00:06:50.689
Systematic reviews, on the other hand, follow
a well-planned methodology to qualitatively

00:06:50.689 --> 00:06:54.729
or quantitatively analyze a defined number
of studies.

00:06:54.729 --> 00:06:59.030
They usually focus on a single question and
have clear study objectives that are worked

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.800
upon in a systematic manner.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:07.229
These studies are based on a well-defined
strategy unlike narrative reviews.

00:07:07.229 --> 00:07:11.809
Systematic reviews and narrative reviews are
organized slightly differently.

00:07:11.809 --> 00:07:15.169
The details are described below:

00:07:15.169 --> 00:07:20.009
Introduction : Systematic reviews begin with
specific research questions that are defined

00:07:20.009 --> 00:07:24.330
in terms of the samples and research outcomes
to be studied .

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:29.159
Methods : These studies have a comprehensive
methodology that starts by narrowing down

00:07:29.159 --> 00:07:31.310
the literature for the review.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:36.839
Usually, specific inclusion and exclusion
criteria are set based on the research questions

00:07:36.839 --> 00:07:40.580
and databases are searched based on these
criteria.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:45.259
Once the sample studies have been shortlisted,
they are analyzed in detail .

00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:51.259
Results : The results section for these studies
involves comprehensive data analysis to determine

00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:54.759
the significance of the study outcomes.

00:07:54.759 --> 00:07:59.150
Systematic reviews can be accompanied with
Meta-analysis which involves statistical analysis

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:02.919
of the included studies to increase the power
of the results.

00:08:02.919 --> 00:08:08.949
and, Discussion : This section usually interprets
the study data based on their weighted significance

00:08:08.949 --> 00:08:11.330
and the power of the results.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:15.360
The study therefore provides strengthened
results that are validated by the scientific

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.249
rigor of the analytical method.

00:08:18.249 --> 00:08:22.449
Before starting to write a review, it is important
to determine what kind of review you want

00:08:22.449 --> 00:08:26.449
to write and follow the appropriate style
and guidelines.

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:30.639
An effective literature review is important
for the complete life cycle of a research

00:08:30.639 --> 00:08:34.620
from defining the right research goals to
correctly interpreting and presenting the

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:39.039
research results.

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:44.159
Let's assume that during the process of literature
review, you have to clearly identify the gap,

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:46.529
but how to concretely fill it.

00:08:46.529 --> 00:08:51.420
You have mapped your scientific literature
after laborious work of detection, reading,

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:52.420
writing.

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:56.170
You have depicted out the problem you want
to resolve or at least make a contribution

00:08:56.170 --> 00:09:00.959
to the literature that has already been studied
by the researcher in your community.

00:09:00.959 --> 00:09:04.709
Then you think it will be interesting to further
the research around this question which needs

00:09:04.709 --> 00:09:06.850
more investigation.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:09.290
And this is what we call the gap.

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:12.370
It's not a matter of has it already been done.

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:16.820
But is it possible to go further in this direction
in an original way?

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:21.630
For example, you should maybe never write
that it has never been done before.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:26.470
Because the reviewer will tell you that maybe
there was a reason or it's just not interesting.

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:30.910
So now you are aware of this, you must explain
in your article how you can really contribute

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:35.160
to the scientific community by filling the
gap in the literature.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.190
We define it as a blank theoretical space
that you really hope at least partially to

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.490
fill thanks to your paper.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:46.550
The logical way to accomplish this after presenting
your gap is to explain your objectives and

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:48.880
how you want to fill this gap.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:52.399
The first step will be to present your research
question.

00:09:52.399 --> 00:09:56.519
The research question of your article will
be the most important sentence of your article

00:09:56.519 --> 00:10:00.320
because it will make the links between the
gaps you will define and how you propose to

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:01.840
fill it.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.300
The research question is the keystone of your
paper.

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:09.649
It has to be clear, precise and concise but
not too general.

00:10:09.649 --> 00:10:13.920
If someone asks what is your article about
and sum it up in a few words, the white line

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.970
research question should be the answer.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:19.089
It should be the one sentence tracking out
your paper.

